# 1. Most Common Command
# The killall command is the standard way to stop all instances of a program by name.
# The killall command is the standard way to stop all instances of a program by name. 
Graceful: killall code # Sends a SIGTERM signal, giving VS Code a chance to save settings and close properly.
Forceful: killall -9 code # Sends a SIGKILL signal, which terminates the processes immediately if they are unresponsive

# 2. Using pkill (Best for Pattern Matching) 
# If killall doesn't find the processes, pkill is more flexible as it can match partial names or full command lines. 
Basic: pkill code # Kills all processes with "code" in the name.
pkill -f .vscode-server # Useful if you are working remotely and need to kill the VS Code server processes specifically.

Command:
nohup python -u script.py > output.log 2>&1 &
Breakdown:
nohup: Keeps the script running even if you close the terminal or log out.
python -u: Runs Python in "unbuffered" mode. This ensures logs are written to the file in real-time rather than being stored in memory first.
> output.log: Redirects standard output to your text file.
2>&1: Redirects error messages (stderr) to the same log file.
&: Puts the process in the background immediately


